{
  "kc": "math.dot-products-norms",
  "problems": [
    {
      "id": 50038,
      "kind": "compute",
      "difficulty": 25,
      "prompt": "Find (1, −2, 3) · (4, 1, −1).",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "9",
          "value": "9"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "(4, −2, −3)",
          "value": "Matrix([4,-2,-3])"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "−1",
          "value": "-1"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "1",
          "value": "1"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "C",
      "solution": "Multiply pairs and add: 4 − 2 − 3 = −1. The tuple is the intermediate products, not their sum.",
      "verify": {
        "sympy": {
          "truth": "Matrix([1,-2,3]).dot(Matrix([4,1,-1]))"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 50039,
      "kind": "compute",
      "difficulty": 25,
      "prompt": "Find the Euclidean norm of (−3, 4).",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "25",
          "value": "25"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "7",
          "value": "7"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "1",
          "value": "1"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "5",
          "value": "5"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "D",
      "solution": "The squared norm is 9 + 16 = 25; take the square root. Adding absolute components gives a different norm.",
      "verify": {
        "sympy": {
          "truth": "sqrt((-3)**2+4**2)"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 50040,
      "kind": "compute",
      "difficulty": 40,
      "prompt": "Which unit vector has the same direction as (0, −3, 4)?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "(0, −3/5, 4/5)",
          "value": "Matrix([0,-Rational(3,5),Rational(4,5)])"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "(0, −3/25, 4/25)",
          "value": "Matrix([0,-Rational(3,25),Rational(4,25)])"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "(0, 3/5, −4/5)",
          "value": "Matrix([0,Rational(3,5),-Rational(4,5)])"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "(0, −1, 1)",
          "value": "Matrix([0,-1,1])"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": "Divide by length 5, not squared length 25. Negating reverses direction; dividing each coordinate by its own magnitude does not normalize Euclidean length.",
      "verify": {
        "sympy": {
          "truth": "Matrix([0,-3,4])/sqrt(9+16)"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 50041,
      "kind": "statement",
      "difficulty": 40,
      "prompt": "u = (2, 1), v = (1, −2). What does u · v = 0 imply?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "Both vectors have zero length."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The two nonzero vectors are perpendicular."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The two vectors are equal."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "They point in the same direction."
        }
      ],
      "answer": "B",
      "solution": "The dot product is 2 − 2 = 0. Nonzero perpendicular vectors have zero dot product; zero dot product does not mean either vector is zero."
    },
    {
